hed Schmitt by 1/100th of a second to put Sweden in the lead on the opening leg. " [:a {:href "/athletes/129061"} "Emma McKeon"] "’s second leg of 1:54.64 gave Australia the lead as she touched 0.31 seconds ahead of Leah Smith, and Sweden dropped all the way back to sixth. " [:a {:href "/athletes/115711"} "Bronte Barratt"] " then outswam DiRado to increase the Australian lead as the anchor leg started, where it was " [:a {:href "/athletes/129068"} "Tamsin Cook"] " against Ledecky, who started with an 0.89 second deficit. She made that up quickly and stormed ahead to what looked like an easy gold medal, although it had not been, winning by almost two seconds over Cook. Canada had been in a battle with China since the second leg, but anchor swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/129511"} "Penny Oleksiak"] " brought them home for the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "United States"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "7:47.77 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "7:43.03 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "10"} [:a {:href "/athletes/115730"} "Allison Schmitt"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/126653"} "Katie Ledecky"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/126679"} "Missy Franklin"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/132157"} "Leah Smith"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/132159"} "Maya DiRado"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/132160"} "Melanie Margalis"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/136355"} "Cierra Runge"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} "Australia"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olymp